        <item>
            <title>FEDS Note: Government Bonds’ Safe-Haven Property and Inflationary Pressures</title>
            <link><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/government-bonds-safe-haven-property-and-inflationary-pressures-20261016.html]]></link>
            <guid><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/government-bonds-safe-haven-property-and-inflationary-pressures-20261016.html]]></guid>
            <description><![CDATA[<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/canlin-li.htm">Canlin Li</a> and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/emre-yoldas.htm">Emre Yoldas</a><a name="f1"></a><br><br>Earlier this year, escalation of the conflict in the Middle East resulted in sharp declines in major stock indexes as energy prices rose and the global economic outlook deteriorated, but prices of long-term government bonds in major advanced economies also declined (Figure 1). This prompted some commentators to question the premise that government bonds in these major advanced economies will rally in times of increased risk aversion and serve as a safe haven for investors (see for example, Kim, 2026).]]></description>
            <category>FEDS Notes</category>
            <pubDate><![CDATA[Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:15:00 GMT]]></pubDate>    
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title>FEDS Note: Ripples in the Reserve Pool: Unveiling Liquidity Signals from the Federal Funds Market</title>
            <link><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/ripples-in-the-reserve-pool-unveiling-liquidity-signals-from-the-federal-funds-market-20261016.html]]></link>
            <guid><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/ripples-in-the-reserve-pool-unveiling-liquidity-signals-from-the-federal-funds-market-20261016.html]]></guid>
            <description><![CDATA[<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/sriya-l-anbil.htm">Sriya Anbil</a>, Sebastian Infante, and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/zeynep-senyuz.htm">Zeynep Senyuz</a><br><br>When the Federal Reserve shifted to an ample-reserves framework, many observers started to question the relevance of the fed funds market. With reserves ample or abundant and rates anchored by administered floors, interbank borrowing declined sharply, and the market appeared to serve mainly as an arbitrage venue for non-bank institutions.]]></description>
            <category>FEDS Notes</category>
            <pubDate><![CDATA[Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:20:00 GMT]]></pubDate>    
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title>FEDS Note: Introducing Private Credit into the Financial Accounts of the United States</title>
            <link><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/introducing-private-credit-into-the-financial-accounts-of-the-united-states-20261016.html]]></link>
            <guid><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/introducing-private-credit-into-the-financial-accounts-of-the-united-states-20261016.html]]></guid>
            <description><![CDATA[Katie Brinkman, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/christine-l-dobridge.htm">Christine Dobridge</a>, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/tim-e-dore.htm">Tim Dore</a>, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/simon-glossner.htm">Simon Glossner</a>, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/elizabeth-b-holmquist.htm">Elizabeth Holmquist</a>, Nam Nguyen, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/maria-g-perozek.htm">Maria Perozek</a>, and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/irina-stefanescu.htm">Irina Stefanescu</a><a name="f1"></a><br><br>Over the past decade, private credit has played an increasingly important role in providing funding to middle-market businesses. While there are several definitions of private credit, here we define it as privately negotiated loans to businesses that are originated by nonbank investment funds rather than by traditional financial intermediaries such as banks.]]></description>
            <category>FEDS Notes</category>
            <pubDate><![CDATA[Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:25:00 GMT]]></pubDate>    
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title>FEDS Note: The Rise of Semiliquid Funds for Retail Investors</title>
            <link><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/the-rise-of-semiliquid-funds-for-retail-investors-20261014.html]]></link>
            <guid><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/the-rise-of-semiliquid-funds-for-retail-investors-20261014.html]]></guid>
            <description><![CDATA[Logan George and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/chaehee-shin.htm">Chaehee Shin</a><br><br>Investment funds differ in how investors can access their capital. The redemption rules governing how and when investors can withdraw have evolved endogenously in response to shifting investor demand and regulatory constraints. While the immediate liquidity of open-end mutual funds and exchange-traded funds has long dominated the retail market, the landscape has been evolving over time.]]></description>
            <category>FEDS Notes</category>
            <pubDate><![CDATA[Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:00:00 GMT]]></pubDate>    
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title>FEDS Note: Convenience Yields, Risk Premiums, and the Interpretation of Oil Futures Prices During Times of Geopolitical Conflict</title>
            <link><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/convenience-yields-risk-premiums-and-the-interpretation-of-oil-futures-prices-during-geo-conflict-20261009.html]]></link>
            <guid><![CDATA[https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/convenience-yields-risk-premiums-and-the-interpretation-of-oil-futures-prices-during-geo-conflict-20261009.html]]></guid>
            <description><![CDATA[<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/erik-a-heitfield.htm">Erik Heitfield</a>, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/yang-ho-park.htm">Yang-Ho Park</a> and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/erfan-danesh.htm">Erfan Danesh</a><br><br>Earlier this year the Middle East conflict led to one of the largest global oil supply shocks in history (Bordoff and O&#39;Sullivan, 2026). Past empirical research has found that commodity futures prices provide accurate predictions of future spot prices under normal market conditions (Reeve and Vigfusson, 2011; Chernenko et al., 2004).]]></description>
            <category>FEDS Notes</category>
            <pubDate><![CDATA[Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:30:00 GMT]]></pubDate>    
